---
/**
 * Benchmarks (MASTER_ROADMAP M21, FUTURE_GATES FG-004): methodology before
 * numbers. The only figures shown are the protocol's own verification counts,
 * quoted with their release and source, and clearly labelled as test-suite
 * evidence rather than performance or recovery benchmarks.
 */
import MaturityBadge from '../../components/MaturityBadge.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { sourceUrl } from '../../lib/canonical';

const METRICS = [
  ['Recovery success rate', 'Share of interrupted runs that reach the next verified boundary after a cold continue, with no human briefing.'],
  ['Mean recovery time', 'Wall-clock time from a cold `saipen continue` to the first productive action after an interruption.'],
  ['Manual intervention rate', 'Share of runs that stop at a WAIT, split by WAIT category, so a legitimate boundary is not counted as a failure.'],
  ['Continuation success', 'Share of handoffs between different models or hosts that resume the same ticket at the same phase.'],
  ['Protocol overhead', 'Extra tokens and wall-clock time per ticket spent on checkpoints, reads and validation.'],
  ['Evidence completeness', 'Share of DONE tickets whose LOG evidence satisfies their verify contract at the declared witness level.'],
  ['Conformance pass rate', 'Share of conformance scenarios passing for a given SAIPEN version.'],
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Benchmarks"
  tag="/benchmarks/"
  description="SAIPEN benchmark methodology: what will be measured, how, and why no recovery or performance numbers are published yet."
>
  <h1>Benchmarks</h1>
  <p><MaturityBadge level="draft" note="methodology only — no benchmark results are published" /></p>
  <p>
    A number is only as meaningful as the method behind it: which model and host, which interruptions, what counted
    as recovered, how many runs. This page publishes the method first. Results will appear only when they come from a
    defined, repeatable pipeline — never as a placeholder or a vanity counter.
  </p>

  <h2>Planned metrics</h2>
  <dl class="doclist">
    {
      METRICS.map(([name, def]) => (
        <>
          <dt>{name}</dt>
          <dd>{def}</dd>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>Method requirements</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Each run records the SAIPEN version and commit, the host and its adapter strength, and the model.</li>
    <li>Interruptions are injected deterministically — killed process, exhausted context, unavailable provider — and logged as such.</li>
    <li>"Recovered" means the next agent reached the next verified boundary from the files alone, judged against the ticket's own verify contract.</li>
    <li>Every run's `.saipen/` directory is kept as evidence, so a third party can re-check the verdict.</li>
    <li>Results are published with their sample size and the runs that failed, not only an average.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What exists today: verification counts, not benchmarks</h2>
  <p>
    The SAIPEN repository reports its own test-suite results in release notes. They show how much of the protocol is
    machine-checked; they say nothing about recovery rates in real projects.
  </p>
  <div class="wide-scroll">
    <table class="w-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Release</th>
          <th scope="col">Reported verification</th>
          <th scope="col">Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">8.0.2</th>
          <td>core-unit 4,623 checks, 0 red; scenario suite 1,231 PASS; ruff clean on the tracked surface</td>
          <td>
            <a href={sourceUrl('CHANGELOG.md')} rel="noopener" data-external>CHANGELOG.md</a>
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
